.TH XOT 8 "" "Xproxy" "System administration"
.nh
.ad l
.SH "NAME"
.PP
xot \- OT daemon: the control protocols and the plant\(aqs own infrastructure
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBxot\fR [\fB\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR] [\fB\-validate\fR] [\fB\-version\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
\fBxot\fR is the OT daemon: the one the plant talks to, and the one that belongs at level 3.5 between a process network and everything else. It reads one YAML configuration file (see \fBxproxy.yaml\fR(5)), binds the listeners it describes or takes them from systemd socket activation, and serves until stopped.
.PP
It serves the control protocols \[u2014] \fBmodbus\fR, \fBiec104\fR, \fBs7\fR, \fBmms\fR, \fBbacnet\fR, \fBopcua\fR and \fBcoap\fR \[u2014] and the protocols the field equipment itself speaks: \fBsnmp\fR, \fBtftp\fR, \fBdhcp\fR, \fBdhcp6\fR, \fBsyslog\fR, the \fBntp\fR and \fBntske\fR time gateway, and \fBmqtt\fR for Sparkplug B telemetry. Those last eight are served by \fBxrelay\fR(8) as well, because a data centre runs them too; a listener of one of those kinds belongs to \fBxrelay\fR unless it says \fBdaemon: xot\fR. MQTT is in the set for a narrower reason: the device inventory collects what one daemon saw, so an estate whose device births arrive over Sparkplug needs them on the daemon that serves its Modbus.
.PP
What it does not serve is the point of it being a program of its own. There is no mail parser in this binary, no FTP, no LDAP, no database wire protocol and no AMQP: that code is in \fBxrelay\fR(8), and an attack on the proxy in front of a process network has none of it to work with. It is the same argument the first split was made for, applied to the daemon whose failure mode is a plant.
.PP
A listener of another role in the same file is validated in full and left to its owner, which is what lets the daemons share a set of includes; a listener kind this binary did not link is never bound.
.PP
The process runs as the \fBxot\fR user under the shipped systemd unit and confines itself with Landlock, seccomp and a dropped capability set (see \fBdocs/HARDENING.md\fR). It is controlled at run time through its own management socket by \fBxproxyctl\fR(8).
